\name{gt_example}
\alias{gt_example}
\title{Load Synthetic Examples or Explicit Local Modeling Tables}
\description{Lists eight documented outcome sets or loads their design and
outcome columns. Bundled observations are entirely synthetic and contain no
actual LLM outputs, source-study records, raw texts, or clinical data.}
\usage{
gt_example(name = NULL, coding = c("native", "manuscript"),
  directory = NULL)
}
\arguments{
\item{name}{Outcome-set name, or \code{NULL} to list available sets.}
\item{coding}{\code{"native"}: preserves the declared response types.

\code{"manuscript"}: the legacy numeric-score compatibility option for
seven sets; it does not load or reproduce manuscript observations or results.}
\item{directory}{Optional directory containing example RDS resources or
compatible source CSV files, supplied explicitly as one nonempty path. A file
named \code{<name>.rds} takes precedence over CSV loading. When omitted, the
installed package loads bundled synthetic resources regardless of workspace
variables. User-supplied files retain the user's own provenance.}
}
\value{A catalog data frame when name is \code{NULL}. Otherwise a list
containing \code{data}, outcome names, observation families, design roles,
example name, coding, resource path, and interpretation notes. Bundled
resources also include \code{source_provenance} with \code{data_kind} equal to
\code{"synthetic"}, author, license, and public generator identity.}
\details{
Each bundled set has 576 rows: 24 hypothetical items by three generic
evaluators, two generic prompts, two temperature-setting identifiers, and two
seed identifiers. All labels are generated by deterministic integer arithmetic.
No raw texts, real model identities, observed prevalence, source-study
annotations, or fitted parameters supply the simulated values.

The available sets are:
\itemize{
\item \code{hate_speech}: three ordered categories.
\item \code{mental_health_7L}: seven-code Gaussian working score.
\item \code{mental_health_3L}: three-code Gaussian working score.
\item \code{mental_health_6flag}: six binary flags.
\item \code{mental_health_3group}: three binary OR groups.
\item \code{drug_review}: five-category ordinal holistic sentiment.
\item \code{drug_review_4aspect}: four ordinal aspects.
\item \code{mental_health_nominal}: seven unordered categories.
}
The nominal set has native coding only. The Mental-Health 7L/3L mappings are
Gaussian working-score illustrations under either option; they are not
validated clinical severity scales. Background annotation papers and corpus
references appear in the three task topics. They motivate the task types but
are not sources for the synthetic observations.

For standalone functions, a binding named \code{.gt_example_data_dir} in the
same enclosing environment can configure the resource directory. The source
loader uses this directory in the checkout:
\preformatted{inst/extdata}
Parent environments and callers are not searched. This standalone convention has no effect on the
installed package. An invalid explicit directory raises an error instead of
falling back silently.

Explicit CSV loading accepts these compatible filenames:
\itemize{
\item \file{hate_labeling_final.csv}
\item \file{mh_labeling_final.csv}
\item \file{drug_labeling_final.csv}
}
This compatibility interface does not make any
such source file part of the bundled examples. RDS files carry their own
provenance. Use \code{\link{gt_preflight}} to inspect fitting restrictions;
successful loading alone is not a numerical or statistical validation.
}
\seealso{\code{\link{gtheory_datasets}}, \code{\link{gtheory_hate_speech}},
\code{\link{gtheory_mental_health}}, \code{\link{gtheory_drug_review}},
\code{\link{gt_family}}, \code{\link{gt_fit}}}
\examples{
gt_example()
x <- gt_example("hate_speech")
nrow(x$data)
is.ordered(x$data$score)
x$source_provenance$data_kind
nominal <- gt_example("mental_health_nominal")
levels(nominal$data$label)
}
